WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.336
This lesson adds a geometric idea to the force story.

2
00:00:05.336 --> 00:00:10.672
A push can be large, but if it acts very close to the pivot its turning effect may still be modest.

3
00:00:10.672 --> 00:00:16.008
A smaller push applied farther away can create the same turning effect.

4
00:00:16.008 --> 00:00:21.344
That is why Spin Pull is not force alone.

5
00:00:21.344 --> 00:00:26.680
It is torque, also called the moment of a force, and it depends on both the force size and the perpendicular reach to the pivot.

6
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:32.016
Keep this takeaway in view: Turning effect belongs to force and perpendicular reach together, not to force alone.

7
00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:37.352
Force means push size.

8
00:00:37.352 --> 00:00:42.688
A larger push can increase turning effect, but force alone is not enough.

9
00:00:42.688 --> 00:00:48.024
Reach means perpendicular distance.

10
00:00:48.024 --> 00:00:53.360
Distance from the pivot matters only in the perpendicular sense.

11
00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:00.939
The spin-pull diagram shows why comparing force size alone can be misleading.

12
00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:08.517
A stronger push closer to the pivot and a weaker push farther away can produce the same turning effect.

13
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:16.096
The picture helps because the geometry becomes visible: the pivot is fixed, the push line is visible, and the effective reach is read perpendicularly.

14
00:01:16.096 --> 00:01:23.674
This is the moment idea in a single image.

15
00:01:23.674 --> 00:01:31.253
Keep this takeaway in view: Torque comparison begins with the pivot and the perpendicular distance, not just the arrow length.

16
00:01:31.253 --> 00:01:38.831
Near push: A larger force can still give only a moderate torque if applied close to the pivot.

17
00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:46.410
Far push: A smaller force can match the torque when applied farther away.

18
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:51.290
A door-handle analogy works because everyone has felt it.

19
00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:56.170
Push near the hinge and the door mostly resists.

20
00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:01.050
Push at the handle, much farther from the hinge, and the same push becomes much more effective at turning the door.

21
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:05.930
The reason is not mysterious.

22
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:10.810
The perpendicular reach has become larger.

23
00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:15.690
That simple experience is the physical meaning behind torque, the moment of a force.

24
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:20.570
Keep this takeaway in view: Torque is the physics version of why a door handle works better than a push near the hinge.

25
00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:25.450
Near hinge means small turning effect.

26
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.330
The push has little leverage because the reach is small.

27
00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:35.210
At handle means large turning effect.

28
00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:40.090
The same push gains leverage because the reach is larger.

29
00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:45.313
The formal relation is torque equals force times perpendicular distance from the pivot.

30
00:02:45.313 --> 00:02:50.537
This is also called the moment of a force.

31
00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:55.760
The word perpendicular matters.

32
00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.983
It is not just any distance along the object.

33
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:06.207
It is the shortest distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force.

34
00:03:06.207 --> 00:03:11.430
That is why the equation should always be read with the geometry in mind rather than as a blind multiplication rule.

35
00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:16.653
Keep this takeaway in view: Torque calculations only make sense when the pivot and perpendicular reach are clearly identified.

36
00:03:16.653 --> 00:03:21.877
Pivot: The turning point must be identified before the distance can be chosen.

37
00:03:21.877 --> 00:03:27.100
Perpendicular reach: This is the effective lever arm for the force.

38
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:32.323
torque = force x perpendicular distance.

39
00:03:32.323 --> 00:03:37.547
moment of a force = F x d_perp.

40
00:03:37.547 --> 00:03:42.770
Use the perpendicular reach from the pivot to the line of action of the force.

41
00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:47.382
Suppose one push is six newtons at zero point five metres and another is three newtons at one metre.

42
00:03:47.382 --> 00:03:51.994
Both products give three newton metres.

43
00:03:51.994 --> 00:03:56.605
So the turning effect is the same.

44
00:03:56.605 --> 00:04:01.217
This matters because it trains learners to compare the full product.

45
00:04:01.217 --> 00:04:05.829
The biggest force number is not always the biggest torque.

46
00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:10.441
The geometry can compensate or dominate.

47
00:04:10.441 --> 00:04:15.053
Keep this takeaway in view: Compare the whole force-distance product before deciding which turning effect is larger.

48
00:04:15.053 --> 00:04:19.665
Case A means 6 x 0.5 = 3 N m.

49
00:04:19.665 --> 00:04:24.276
Stronger push, shorter reach.

50
00:04:24.276 --> 00:04:28.888
Case B means 3 x 1.0 = 3 N m.

51
00:04:28.888 --> 00:04:33.500
Smaller push, longer reach.

52
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:39.139
A common trap is to measure along the bar instead of to the force line perpendicularly.

53
00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:44.778
Another trap is to choose the larger force without checking the reach.

54
00:04:44.778 --> 00:04:50.416
Both mistakes ignore the geometry that makes torque meaningful.

55
00:04:50.416 --> 00:04:56.055
A third subtle trap is to forget that a force through the pivot can still push the object without producing any turning effect at all.

56
00:04:56.055 --> 00:05:01.694
Translation and rotation must be separated carefully.

57
00:05:01.694 --> 00:05:07.332
Keep this takeaway in view: Torque questions are geometry questions as much as force questions.

58
00:05:07.332 --> 00:05:12.971
Mark the pivot first.

59
00:05:12.971 --> 00:05:18.610
Use the perpendicular reach to the force line, not just any length.

60
00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:22.133
Check yourself.

61
00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:25.656
What two things determine torque?

62
00:05:25.656 --> 00:05:29.179
Force size and perpendicular reach from the pivot.

63
00:05:29.179 --> 00:05:32.702
What is another name for torque?

64
00:05:32.702 --> 00:05:36.225
The moment of a force.

65
00:05:36.225 --> 00:05:39.748
Can a smaller force produce the same turning effect as a larger force?

66
00:05:39.748 --> 00:05:43.272
Yes, if it acts farther from the pivot so the product matches.

67
00:05:43.272 --> 00:05:46.795
Those three answers capture the whole lesson core.

68
00:05:46.795 --> 00:05:50.318
Keep this takeaway in view: Torque becomes simple when the pivot geometry is kept visible.

69
00:05:50.318 --> 00:05:53.841
Determinants means F and d_perp.

70
00:05:53.841 --> 00:05:57.364
Turning effect needs both push size and effective reach.

71
00:05:57.364 --> 00:06:00.887
Formal name means moment of a force.

72
00:06:00.887 --> 00:06:04.410
This is the standard physics term for torque.

73
00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:10.305
You are ready for the worked example now.

74
00:06:10.305 --> 00:06:16.200
Compare two pushes: six newtons applied zero point five metres from the hinge and three newtons applied one point zero metres from the hinge.

75
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:22.095
Use torque equals force times perpendicular distance.

76
00:06:22.095 --> 00:06:27.990
The first gives six times zero point five, which is three newton metres.

77
00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:33.885
The second gives three times one point zero, which is also three newton metres.

78
00:06:33.885 --> 00:06:39.780
So both pushes give the same Spin Pull, or torque, because the products match exactly.

79
00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:45.675
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: calculate the moment of a force, then compare the turning effects directly.

80
00:06:45.675 --> 00:06:51.570
Step 1: Identify the pivot and the perpendicular reach for each push.

81
00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:57.465
Step 2: Calculate torque for each case using force times perpendicular distance.

82
00:06:57.465 --> 00:07:03.360
6 x 0.5 = 3 N m.

83
00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.255
3 x 1.0 = 3 N m.

84
00:07:09.255 --> 00:07:15.150
Different force-distance pairs can produce the same moment of a force.
